            <ArticleTitle>Cardiac tamponade likely due to candida infection, in an immunocompetent patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>122-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ancard.2011.07.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0003-3928(11)00112-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Candida pericarditis is a rare disease described mainly in immunodepressed patients. Here we report the case of a 76-year-old immunocompetent woman who developed a purulent pericarditis 48 hours after pericardiocentesis. Usual etiologies such as cancer or pericardo-oesophageal fistula, were ruled out. Physical examination revealed a sub-mammary mycosis, which could have led to the infection. The early diagnosis and treatment with a combined medical and surgical approach succeeded in a favorable evolution of this case.</AbstractText>
